  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY NIGHT THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 316 AM EDT Thu Jul 30 2026\n\nThe mid/upper level trough rotates southeast over Iowa and Illinois \nFriday night into Saturday, with the low closing off in the mid-\nlevels. The sfc low should occlude over Illinois by Saturday \nmorning. A developing 25-30 kt SW LLJ will enhance low-level WAA and \nmoisture transport ahead of the low late Friday night, and \nespecially into Saturday morning. A round of showers and elevated \nstorms appears likely early Saturday. The severe weather risk will \nbe very low at this time. \n\nNumerous rain showers are expected on Saturday, and it's not clear \nhow much of a window we'll have to destabilize. A 30 kt SW LLJ will \ncontinue to pump warm, moist low-level air into the region Saturday \nafternoon as the sfc low spins over southern IL. Sfc dewpoints are \nforecast to rise into the low to mid 70s, and pockets of moderate \ndestabilization (MLCAPE 1000-1500 J/kg) are certainly possible. \n\nEffective deep shear of 25-30 kts will be marginal for organized \nconvection, though the enhanced lower level wind fields should at \nleast boost the potential for isolated strong wind gusts. Backed sfc \nwinds out of the S/SSE just ahead of the low does enhanced low-level \ncurvature on forecast hodographs. Forecast low-level shear and SRH \nare still fairly modest, however. \n\nStorm mode is currently expected to be a mix of loosely organized \nmulticells, with embedded supercellular structure. Overall, it \nappears to be a conditional risk for severe weather on Saturday. \nForecast confidence in severe storms is low-medium. Isolated \ndamaging winds are likely the primary threat, with a lower risk for \na tornado or two. \n\nLREF mean PW values are forecast to increase into the 1.8-2.0 inch \nrange on Saturday. The moist environment will be supportive of heavy \nrainfall rates of 1-2 inches/hr. Locally heavy rainfall could result \nin flash flooding. Average rainfall amounts of 1-2 inches look \npossible, though locally higher totals will be possible due to \nconvection. \n\nStacked low pressure drifts overhead on Sunday, likely allowing \nlighter showery weather to continue. The weakening cutoff mid-level \nlow may meander over the TN Valley through midweek, while the sfc \nboundary stalls and slowly washes out over the eastern CONUS. A \nchance for scattered showers and storms will linger Monday through \nWednesday, especially during the afternoon and evening hours.",
